Author: Pavithra R <pavir@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date:   Sun Jun 22 00:00:00 2026 +0530

[mcproxy] mroute_socket: Downgrade get_mroute_stats ioctl error log from ERROR to DEBUG

When SIOCGETSGCNT / SIOCGETSGCNT_IN6 returns errno 99 (EADDRNOTAVAIL),
it means the requested (S,G) entry is not present in the kernel multicast
routing table.  This is a transient, expected condition — the route may
have just been torn down, timed out, or not yet been installed — and is
not a programming error.

The continuous stream of "failed to get multicast route stats!
Error: Address not available errno: 99" messages on the console is
therefore misleading.  Downgrade both ioctl failure paths from HC_LOG_ERROR
to HC_LOG_DEBUG so the noise disappears in validation while remaining visible
with debug logging enabled.  The function already returns false on this path,
which is the correct action for the caller to handle gracefully.

Signed-off-by: Pavithra R <pavir@qti.qualcomm.com>
diff -Nur a/mcproxy/src/utils/mroute_socket.cpp b/mcproxy/src/utils/mroute_socket.cpp
--- a/mcproxy/src/utils/mroute_socket.cpp
+++ b/mcproxy/src/utils/mroute_socket.cpp
@@ -779,7 +779,7 @@ bool mroute_socket::get_mroute_stats(const addr_storage& source_addr, const add

             rc = ioctl(m_sock, SIOCGETSGCNT, sgreq_v4);;
             if (rc == -1) {
-                HC_LOG_ERROR("failed to get multicast route stats! Error: " << strerror(errno) << " errno: " << errno);
+                HC_LOG_DEBUG("failed to get multicast route stats! Error: " << strerror(errno) << " errno: " << errno);
                 return false;
             } else {
                 return true;
@@ -798,7 +798,7 @@ bool mroute_socket::get_mroute_stats(const addr_storage& source_addr, const add

             rc = ioctl(m_sock, SIOCGETSGCNT_IN6, sgreq_v6);
             if (rc == -1) {
-                HC_LOG_ERROR("failed to get multicast route stats! Error: " << strerror(errno) << " errno: " << errno);
+                HC_LOG_DEBUG("failed to get multicast route stats! Error: " << strerror(errno) << " errno: " << errno);
                 return false;
             } else {
                 return true;
